<p>An <strong>SEO website audit</strong> is a structured way to investigate why a site may not be visible for its target searches. Use this hub to choose the relevant diagnostic or sector guide; use the <a href="/seo-website-audit/how-to/">DIY procedure</a> for execution and the <a href="/seo-website-audit/full-audit/">full-audit guide</a> to define a professional deliverable.</p>

<p>Many SEO issues are invisible to the naked eye. You cannot see a missing canonical tag, a crawl budget problem or keyword cannibalisation by browsing your own site. A structured audit process helps surface and document those observations.</p>

<p>The sections below give a concise orientation to scope, evidence and prioritisation. Follow the linked specialist pages when you need platform detail, a diagnostic sequence or an implementation-ready workflow.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-an-seo-website-audit">What Is an SEO Website Audit</h2>

<p>An SEO website audit reviews the search visibility signals included in the agreed scope. Unlike a general <a href="/website-audit/">website audit</a> that covers UX, security and accessibility, an SEO audit focuses on the on-page, technical and off-page signals used by search engines.</p>

<p>The audit produces a report that documents observed issues, explains why they matter and recommends a specific fix. Where the inputs support it, the report can include a transparent impact estimate with its assumptions.</p>
<p>See the <a href="/seo-website-audit/report/">SEO audit report guide</a> for the structure and interpretation of that deliverable.</p>

<p>There are three core pillars of an SEO audit:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>On-page SEO</strong> — Everything on the page itself: content quality, keyword targeting, title tags, headings, internal links, images, and structured data</li>
  <li><strong>Technical SEO</strong> — The infrastructure that determines whether search engines can discover, crawl, render, and index your content correctly</li>
  <li><strong>Off-page SEO</strong> — External signals of authority and trust, primarily backlinks and brand mentions</li>
</ol>

<p>A complete SEO audit examines all three pillars. Focusing on just one — which is a common mistake — leaves major opportunities on the table and can lead you to misdiagnose the root cause of ranking problems.</p>

<h2 id="why-you-need-one">Why You Need One</h2>

<p>Organic search can be an important acquisition channel, but visibility depends on technical access, page relevance, content quality and external signals. An audit helps identify which of those areas needs investigation.</p>

<h3>You Cannot Fix What You Cannot See</h3>

<p>SEO issues are technical by nature. A missing hreflang tag, a misconfigured canonical, a JavaScript rendering problem, or a crawl trap created by faceted navigation — none of these are visible when you browse your site normally. They only show up in crawl data, log files, and search console reports. An audit is the process of systematically checking all of these data sources.</p>

<h3>Google's Algorithm Changes Constantly</h3>

<p>Google changes its search systems over time and publishes major ranking updates on its <a href="https://status.search.google.com/products/rGHU1u87FJnkP6W2GwMi/history">Search Status Dashboard</a>. When performance changes, compare dates and affected queries or pages before attributing the change to an update.</p>

<h3>Competitors Are Not Standing Still</h3>

<p>Your competitors are publishing content, earning backlinks, and improving their technical foundations. Even if your site has not changed, your relative position in search results can decline simply because competitors have improved. An audit benchmarks your site against the current competitive landscape.</p>

<h3>Technical Debt Accumulates</h3>

<p>Every plugin update, CMS migration, content management change, and developer deployment can introduce SEO issues. Redirect chains grow longer. Orphaned pages multiply. Internal link equity gets diluted. Without regular audits, technical debt compounds until it becomes a serious drag on performance.</p>

<h3>How Should You Value Audit Fixes?</h3>

<p>SEO audit findings can reveal barriers to search visibility and help teams choose a defensible order for implementation. Any traffic or revenue effect requires a dated comparison after the changes and should be reported as measured or estimated.</p>

<p>If the reason for the audit is a sudden visibility change, start with the <a href="/seo-website-audit/rankings-drop/">rankings-drop diagnosis guide</a> before treating every issue as a general optimisation task.</p>

<h2 id="key-areas-to-check">Key Areas to Check</h2>

<p>Before diving into the specifics of each check, here is an overview of the major areas an SEO audit should cover and why each one matters.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Crawlability</strong> — Crawl access affects what Google can fetch and process. Check robots.txt, XML sitemaps, crawl errors, and JavaScript rendering.</li>
  <li><strong>Indexation</strong> — Crawled pages are not automatically indexed. Check for noindex tags, canonical issues, duplicate content, and index coverage in Google Search Console.</li>
  <li><strong>Site architecture</strong> — How your pages link to each other determines how link equity flows and how easily users and search engines can discover content. Check click depth, orphan pages, and hub-spoke structures.</li>
  <li><strong>On-page optimisation</strong> — Each page needs a unique, optimised title tag, meta description, heading structure, and content that matches search intent for its target keywords.</li>
  <li><strong>Content quality</strong> — Review whether content is helpful, reliable and people-first, and record relevant evidence of experience, expertise, authoritativeness and trustworthiness. These are assessment dimensions rather than a standalone E-E-A-T score.</li>
  <li><strong>Page speed</strong> — Review Core Web Vitals as user-experience evidence. Slow pages can affect usability and conversions, but a performance threshold miss is not proof of ranking loss.</li>
  <li><strong>Mobile experience</strong> — Google uses mobile-first indexing, meaning it evaluates the mobile version of your site for ranking purposes.</li>
  <li><strong>Backlink profile</strong> — Review referring pages and domains, context, acquisition history and lost links. Counts alone do not establish authority or explain a ranking change.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="on-page-seo-checks">On-Page SEO Checks</h2>

<p>On-page SEO is where most quick wins live. These are the factors you have complete control over, and fixes can be implemented immediately.</p>

<h3>Title Tags</h3>

<p>A title tag should be unique, concise, descriptive and aligned with the page's intent. Include important terms naturally and remember that Google may truncate or rewrite the displayed title, so character counts are practical guides rather than hard limits.</p>

<p>Common title tag issues we find in audits:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Duplicate title tags across multiple pages</li>
  <li>Title tags that are too long and get truncated</li>
  <li>Generic titles like "Home" or "Page 1" that waste ranking potential</li>
  <li>Missing title tags entirely (more common than you would think)</li>
  <li>Keyword stuffing that triggers spam filters</li>
</ul>

<h3>Meta Descriptions</h3>

<p>Meta descriptions are not a direct ranking factor. Write a unique, accurate summary that represents the page content; Google may use other text for the snippet and displayed length varies by result.</p>

<h3>Heading Structure</h3>

<p>Your H1 should match the page's primary topic and include the target keyword. H2s should break the content into logical sections that cover sub-topics. H3s and below provide further structure within sections. Common issues include multiple H1 tags, skipped heading levels, and headings that do not align with the content hierarchy.</p>

<h3>Internal Linking</h3>

<p>Internal links are one of the most underused SEO levers. They distribute link equity, establish topical relationships, and help search engines understand your site structure. Audit for orphaned pages that have no internal links pointing to them, pages with excessive links that dilute equity, and missing contextual links between related content.</p>

<h3>Image Optimisation</h3>

<p>Provide descriptive alt text for informative images and use an empty alt value for decorative images. Use descriptive file names, explicit dimensions to reduce layout shifts, and modern formats such as WebP or AVIF where they suit the asset.</p>

<h3>Structured Data</h3>

<p>Structured data can identify visible content for machine consumers, but valid markup does not guarantee a Google rich result. Validate general Schema.org vocabulary with the Schema Markup Validator and use Google's Rich Results Test only for types Google currently supports; HowTo has no Google rich-result benefit and FAQ results are largely limited to authoritative government and health sites.</p>

<h3>Content Quality and Depth</h3>

<p>For each target search task, review current results to understand likely intent and required evidence, then create the clearest accurate answer your audience needs. Do not copy competitor length or structure: add first-party evidence, expert explanation or practical examples where they improve the answer.</p>

<h3>Keyword Cannibalisation</h3>

<p>Multiple URLs appearing for one query is a signal to investigate, not proof of cannibalisation. Compare intent, content, canonical signals and page performance in Search Console before consolidating or differentiating targeting.</p>

<h2 id="technical-seo-checks">Technical SEO Checks</h2>

<p>Technical SEO issues are often the highest-impact findings in an audit because they can affect hundreds or thousands of pages at once.</p>

<h3>Crawlability</h3>

<p>Start by checking your robots.txt file for any unintended disallow rules. Then review your XML sitemap to ensure it includes all indexable pages and excludes non-indexable ones. Check Google Search Console's Crawl Stats report for any crawl errors, and review server logs if available to see exactly how Googlebot interacts with your site.</p>

<h3>Indexation</h3>

<p>Use Google Search Console's <a href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/7440203">Page indexing report</a> to review indexed and excluded URLs. For a specific page, use <a href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/9012289">URL Inspection</a> to compare Google's indexed view with a live test. The live test does not check every indexing condition and cannot guarantee inclusion. Compare sitemap URLs with the intended indexable URL set as an implementation check rather than as a direct indexed-page count.</p>

<h3>Site Speed</h3>

<p>Test your most important pages with PageSpeed Insights and focus on the three Core Web Vitals. LCP should be under 2.5 seconds, INP under 200 milliseconds, and CLS under 0.1. Fix the largest contributors first — typically large unoptimised images, render-blocking scripts, and slow server response times.</p>

<h3>Mobile Usability</h3>

<p>With mobile-first indexing, review the mobile version of your site. As a practical guide, provide touch targets around 44–48 CSS pixels with enough spacing, then test text, scrolling and interaction on a real device.</p>

<h3>HTTPS and Security</h3>

<p>Every page should load over HTTPS with a valid SSL certificate. Check for mixed content (HTTP resources loaded on HTTPS pages), HTTP-to-HTTPS redirect chains, and certificate expiration dates. Missing HTTPS is both a ranking negative and a trust signal issue.</p>

<h3>Redirect Chains</h3>

<p>Redirect chains occur when URL A redirects to URL B, which redirects to URL C. Each hop adds latency and complicates crawling and debugging. Audit for chains longer than one redirect and update internal links and sitemaps to point directly to the final destination URL.</p>

<h3>Duplicate Content</h3>

<p>Duplicate content can arise from URL parameters, www vs non-www versions, HTTP vs HTTPS, trailing slashes, and CMS-generated variations. Where duplicates exist, ensure canonical signals accurately identify the preferred version and check which canonical Google selected.</p>

<h2 id="off-page-seo-checks">Off-Page SEO Checks</h2>

<p>An off-page scope can review external links and brand mentions as discovery and authority evidence. Interpret referring pages, context and acquisition history rather than treating a domain count as a ranking score.</p>

<h3>Backlink Quality</h3>

<p>Backlinks differ in relevance and context. Review referring pages and domains for unrelated patterns, paid or manipulative schemes and links that need investigation. A third-party toxicity score is not by itself evidence of a search penalty.</p>

<h3>Anchor Text Distribution</h3>

<p>Review the mix and context of anchor text, including branded terms, URLs, generic phrases and descriptive terms. Interpret patterns alongside the linking pages and acquisition history instead of applying an arbitrary percentage threshold.</p>

<h3>Referring Domain Diversity</h3>

<p>Record unique referring domains alongside the linking pages, context, industries and geographies represented. Do not infer a ranking effect from the count alone; interpret changes with the site's search and link history.</p>

<h3>Lost and Broken Backlinks</h3>

<p>Check for backlinks pointing to pages that return 404 errors. Fix the referring link or redirect it only when a relevant replacement exists. Also monitor recently lost backlinks and record whether the referring site removed the link or changed its content.</p>

<h3>Competitor Backlink Gap</h3>

<p>Compare your backlink profile against the top three competitors for your primary keywords. Identify domains that link to multiple competitors but not to you — these represent the most realistic link building opportunities because the sites have already demonstrated a willingness to link to content in your space.</p>

<h2 id="tools-for-seo-audits">Tools for SEO Audits</h2>

<p>Running a thorough SEO audit requires several tools, each serving a different purpose.</p>

<h3>Essential Tools (Free)</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Google Search Console</strong> — The authoritative source for how Google sees your site. Indexation data, query performance, Core Web Vitals, and manual actions.</li>
  <li><strong>Google PageSpeed Insights</strong> — Core Web Vitals testing with both lab and field data.</li>
  <li><strong>Screaming Frog SEO Spider (free up to 500 URLs)</strong> — Desktop crawler that captures title tags, meta descriptions, headings, status codes, and internal links.</li>
  <li><strong>Google Lighthouse</strong> — Built into Chrome DevTools. Audits performance, accessibility, best practices, and SEO.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Professional Tools (Paid)</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Ahrefs</strong> — Paid platform for site audits, keyword research, backlink analysis, and rank tracking. Its Site Audit tool crawls a site and categorises issues by severity.</li>
  <li><strong>Semrush</strong> — Paid platform with site audit, keyword research, backlink and rank-tracking features. Compare coverage and workflow against your scope.</li>
  <li><strong>Screaming Frog (paid)</strong> — Removes the 500 URL limit and adds features like custom extraction, Google Analytics integration, and crawl comparison.</li>
  <li><strong>Sitebulb</strong> — Visual site audit tool that excels at identifying site architecture issues and presenting data in an intuitive way.</li>
</ul>

<p>For a detailed comparison of all audit tools, see our <a href="/website-audit-tools/">best website audit tools</a> guide.</p>

<h2 id="how-to-prioritise-fixes">How to Prioritise Fixes</h2>

<p>Most SEO audits produce a long list of issues. Trying to fix everything at once is overwhelming and inefficient. Here is how to prioritise for maximum impact.</p>

<h3>The Impact-Effort Matrix</h3>

<p>Score every issue on two dimensions: impact (how much it affects rankings and traffic) and effort (how long it takes to fix). This creates four quadrants:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Quick wins</strong> (high impact, low effort) — Fix these first. Examples: adding missing title tags, fixing broken internal links, implementing canonical tags.</li>
  <li><strong>Major projects</strong> (high impact, high effort) — Schedule these for the medium term. Examples: site migration, content overhaul, link building campaign.</li>
  <li><strong>Fill-ins</strong> (low impact, low effort) — Do these when you have spare capacity. Examples: optimising alt text, fixing minor redirect chains.</li>
  <li><strong>Deprioritise</strong> (low impact, high effort) — Skip these unless everything else is done. Examples: rebuilding a legacy URL structure with minimal traffic.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Revenue-First Prioritisation</h3>

<p>Use business relevance when the required inputs are available. Document the baseline, assumptions and confidence for any traffic or revenue estimate, and keep measured outcomes separate from forecasts.</p>

<h3>Dependency Mapping</h3>

<p>Some fixes are prerequisites for others. For example, fixing crawlability issues should come before optimising on-page content, because there is no point optimising pages that Google cannot find. Map dependencies and execute in the right order.</p>

<h3>Track Progress</h3>

<p>After implementing fixes, re-crawl the affected scope and repeat the finding's verification check. Monitor Search Console and business outcomes from the implementation date, allowing for recrawling and normal variance. Schedule the next review from release rate and risk rather than a fixed 30–60-day rule.</p>

<p>Use the <a href="/resources/agency-audit-toolkit/">agency audit toolkit</a> to record evidence, owners and verification criteria. If you want professional help prioritising and implementing SEO audit fixes, our <a href="/website-audit-services/">audit services</a> include a prioritised action plan for the agreed scope.</p>

